    "knowledge_point": "Improper Integrals Involving Arctangent Using the Second Result",
    "problem": "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{{z}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}z.$",
    "worked_solution": "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{k}^{2}+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{k})+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{{z}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}z & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}+{3}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{z}{3})]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{3})-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{3})]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/assets/2465f099a94aa966dbf33eb89aa00973e692fa97c887a9b79baff8260f5d31cd.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{{z}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{3})] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
